60,3,1,talk,Shizue,,"Looking at our options, I feel the Laboratory Ventilation Upgrade is the safest choice. It sounds like an essential improvement, and as we discussed, those necessary works are much more likely to stay on schedule.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Yumi,,"@Shizue, I see your point! But wait, wouldn't that close the labs? I'm worried we'd have to pay for off-site space again! Maybe the Central Study Commons is better since it uses an underused hall?","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,George,,"@Yumi, while I understand your concern about space, the Laboratory Ventilation Upgrade is the only option that directly addresses the manual burdens of our technicians. It is a necessary investment in our people.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I agree that the ventilation upgrade helps staff, but we must be cautious. @Mizuki's findings showed that ground-floor work in older blocks often hits hazardous materials, which could cause massive delays and budget overruns.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"@Mizuki, a keen observation. However, the real danger with Option D is the mechanical plant. Given the twenty percent surge in those costs, a full ventilation overhaul is a financial gamble we cannot afford.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Shizue,,"Oh dear, it seems Option D is more complicated than I first thought. @Benjamin, if the costs are so volatile, perhaps we should look at the Central Study Commons? It seems much simpler and less risky.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"I think the Central Study Commons is a wonderful idea! It uses a space that's already underused, so it won't mess up the timetable or force us to hire expensive off-site rooms! Everyone would love it!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,George,,"@Yumi, while a study space is pleasant, it does nothing for the staff struggling with outdated equipment. We cannot continue to ignore the technicians' daily burdens just because a project is ""simpler.""","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I sympathize, but we must be logical. Option C provides the shared, bookable utility that my data shows users value most, without the high risk of hazardous materials or skyrocketing plant costs found in Option D.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@George, while your commitment to the staff is commendable, we must not confuse necessity with viability. Option C avoids the volatile mechanical markets and the missing reserves that would make Option D a fiscal nightmare.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Shizue,,"It is so heartening to see us finding common ground. @George, I truly wish we could help the technicians, but I fear a failed project helps no one. Option C seems the most peaceful path forward.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Yumi,,I'm so glad we're agreeing! Option C is just perfect because it gives us more flexible space for everyone without the stress of closing rooms. It's a win-win for the students and the budget!,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,George,,"I cannot simply agree to a ""win-win"" that leaves our technicians behind. If we choose Option C, we are once again prioritizing convenience over the people who keep this university running. We must be brave enough to invest in Option D.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, bravery without a plan is just recklessness. Option D risks massive delays and budget failure. Option C is a proven model for success—shared, high-utility space that benefits the entire campus community without risking a total collapse.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@George, bravery is a virtue, but in accounting, it is often a synonym for negligence. Option C is the only choice that respects both our current budget and the reality of the market. Logic dictates we choose stability.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Shizue,,"I feel we have reached a gentle consensus. While my heart goes out to the technicians, we must be honest about our limits. I will vote for Option C to ensure a successful, timely completion.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Yumi,,"I'm with you, @Shizue! Option C is the best way to help the most people without causing a timetable disaster. I'll vote for the Central Study Commons too! Let's make it happen!","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Shizue""],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,George,,"I cannot in good conscience join a consensus that ignores the exhaustion of our staff. I will vote for Option D, as it is the only plan that provides the relief our technicians desperately need.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,I must prioritize a project that is both viable and highly valued by the community. Option C avoids the risks of hazardous materials and budget overruns while providing the shared utility users desire. I vote for Option C.,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a rare pleasure when logic and pragmatism align so neatly. To avoid a fiscal fantasy and ensure we deliver a project that actually exists upon completion, I also vote for Option C.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
